WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:08.400
On August 24, 2020, as COVID-19 cases were&nbsp;
spiking across the state, the University of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.200
Illinois welcomed students back to campus&nbsp;
at Urbana Champaign for the fall semester.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.880
The decision was informed by&nbsp;
months of planning and research.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:21.120
According to an epidemiological model&nbsp;
developed by university professors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.360
there could be a relatively safe way&nbsp;
to open campus for in-person classes.

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:28.640
NEWS: They’re doing 10,000 to 15,000 tests a day.

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:33.440
NEWS: We're testing everybody on our&nbsp;
campus at Urbana-Champaign twice a week.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:35.200
NEWS: Results can be accessed on an app.

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.680
Things didn’t go according&nbsp;
to plan. On September first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.120
just a week after students returned&nbsp;
to campus, there was an outbreak.

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:47.200
NEWS: Two weeks in, the University of&nbsp;
Illinois reported more than 700 cases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.760
which is more than they expected for the semester.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:52.240
And the University of Illinois wasn’t alone.

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:55.520
Tucker Carlson: At this point, we should not&nbsp;
be surprised that the model got it wrong.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:01:01.040
Andrew Cuomo: It sounds like I’m saying I don’t&nbsp;
really trust the experts… because I don’t.

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.920
Why does it seem like COVID-19&nbsp;
models are getting it so wrong?

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:10.000
In reality, there’s a lot more going on here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:16.000
In order to understand how models can be useful,&nbsp;
we first have to understand how they’re built,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.680
what they can— and can’t—tell us, and how&nbsp;
all of this often gets lost in translation.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:26.400
Epidemiology is not rocket science—it's harder.

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:36.560
When scientists build an epidemiological&nbsp;
model, what they’re really doing is writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.200
down a recipe for how they think&nbsp;
the reality of an epidemic unfolds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.720
and adding math so that they can project&nbsp;
that reality into different possible futures.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:49.840
Typically, in a model, what&nbsp;
you'll do is first write down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:52.960
what we call the variables. These are&nbsp;
the things that are changing over time.

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.640
And then in the next step, you’re going to try and&nbsp;
write down how those different variables interact.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.440
And then what you hope to do is take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.920
some observations about the real world&nbsp;
and help to parameterize your model.

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:09.520
How fast does the disease spread&nbsp;
from one person to another?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:15.680
How many contacts does a typical person of&nbsp;
some age have with people of other ages?

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.800
Scientists use two main approaches&nbsp;
to building models of COVID-19:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.440 --> 00:02:26.560
agent-based and compartmental models.&nbsp;
Most models are a combination of both.

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:32.480
With an agent-based model, our goal&nbsp;
is to think about individual agents—in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.760
this case, people. We give them certain rules or&nbsp;
certain behaviors, and then can simulate forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:42.000
based on those behaviors, how they&nbsp;
interact—and in the case of COVID,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.120
how they spread disease and how their behaviors&nbsp;
might change because of the disease around them.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:52.160
But agent-based models don’t easily scale&nbsp;
to very large populations. They can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.080
require a prohibitive amount of&nbsp;
computational resources to run.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.720 --> 00:03:03.920
So, for a broader perspective, scientists&nbsp;
also use compartmental models, or SEIR models.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:08.560
Everyone in the population falls&nbsp;
into one of these categories “S,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.920
for susceptible to the disease, “E,” for&nbsp;
exposed, “I” for infectious, and “R” for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.920
recovered or removed. You can think about these&nbsp;
compartments as different stages of the disease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:26.160
Modelers use differential equations&nbsp;
to simulate how people move from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.240
stage to the next based on data like infection&nbsp;
rate, hospitalization rate, and fatality rate.

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:39.200
Scientists can use approaches like compartmental&nbsp;
and agent-based models to approximate reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:45.840
But reality is a complicated and&nbsp;
unpredictable place, rife with uncertainty.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:49.920
First off, we all go in assuming we're&nbsp;
going to be wrong. We understand that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:56.720
there is no way to get it right. There is&nbsp;
no such thing as a model that will exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:02.800
represent the world or can possibly capture&nbsp;
every one of the dynamics in the real world.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:11.120
The prediction is very sensitive to how good&nbsp;
data is. Data is dispersed, is incomplete,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:18.400
and is not taken with one uniform protocol&nbsp;
everywhere. When you don't have good data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:20.400
you cannot have good predictions.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.600
Small differences in the parameter are amplified&nbsp;
as the dynamics of the system progresses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:31.120
So this is very similar to the butterfly effect.&nbsp;
So slightly different initial conditions leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:36.640
a very different outcome. This exponential&nbsp;
sensitivity causes differences to amplify&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:42.800
exponentially fast. So you have a spreading&nbsp;
cone of possible trajectories of your future.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:51.440
But there’s an even deeper source of uncertainty&nbsp;
that lurks in every model. That source... is us.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.840
So when we look at human behavior&nbsp;
and human dynamics, there's a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:58.160
amount of uncertainty there that you can imagine.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:09.760
Our daily contact patterns are so out of the norm.&nbsp;
We’ve been scrambling to find data, to get insight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:16.080
into how people are behaving during COVID and&nbsp;
how that behavior is changing on a weekly basis.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:20.400
In the absence of data on&nbsp;
relevant human behaviors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.840
modelers have to make assumptions. A small&nbsp;
error or a missed assumption can have profound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:31.280
consequences on the model’s outcome. And even&nbsp;
correct assumptions can change over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.280 --> 00:05:35.040
causing the model to veer way off&nbsp;
course from its original projections.

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:41.760
This is why nobody can predict&nbsp;
the future — not even modelers.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.600
Models fundamentally depend on what&nbsp;
are the assumptions that you make.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:55.520
We made assumptions that people would be partying.&nbsp;
We estimated how much that would be going on.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.520
What we didn't take into account is we didn't&nbsp;
expect that people would be noncompliant.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:08.320
In other words, if you are told to isolate,&nbsp;
we assumed that people would actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.920
do that. That assumption was one of&nbsp;
the most erroneous ones that we made.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:19.840
As a modeler, you have to try to focus&nbsp;
on what are the important things.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:25.120
And they may not be the things that you&nbsp;
would spend most of your time on as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.400
scientist writing a paper for publication.&nbsp;
But if you're doing that, but meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:35.280
you've left out the fact that, you know, a third&nbsp;
of the people in your population don't even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.280 --> 00:06:42.880
believe that you should be even trying to mitigate&nbsp;
COVID—that's a far more important source of error.

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.680
The statistician George E.P. Box once famously&nbsp;
said, “All models are wrong, but some are useful.”

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.600
Instead of predictions, something&nbsp;
modelers can offer us is projections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.800 --> 00:07:01.840
These are glimpses into a range of possible&nbsp;
futures that we have the power to change.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.760
The best early models are the ones that are wrong&nbsp;
precisely because we react to them and make them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:12.640
wrong. So when we see projections about what&nbsp;
could happen where we could do nothing at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:17.360
the fact that those projections are&nbsp;
invariably wrong is fantastic because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.960
that means that we actually respond to the&nbsp;
model's predictions and prove them wrong.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:24.720
But even when a model does&nbsp;
the job it’s supposed to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.840
there’s a risk that it could be misunderstood&nbsp;
when the worst-case scenario doesn’t come to pass.

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:36.240
How do we communicate when we can't predict the&nbsp;
future, but we can provide scenario predictions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.240 --> 00:07:41.520
right? This is a real challenge&nbsp;
for scientists, for modelers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.520
but it's also a really important responsibility.

00:07:43.520 --> 00:07:48.960
So early in the pandemic, one of the&nbsp;
very first requests we received from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.960
policymakers trying to understand what the threat&nbsp;
was and trying to figure out what the right path&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.360
forward was a request from the White House&nbsp;
Coronavirus Task Force. “What do your models&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:05.840
say about how many people are going to die from&nbsp;
COVID-19 in 2020?” And we responded and said, you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:10.720
know, we don't have a crystal ball. It depends on&nbsp;
the policies. It depends on people's behavior. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.720 --> 00:08:16.160
we can't give you a forecast, but we can make some&nbsp;
projections under a number of different scenarios.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:19.680
For example, we can make&nbsp;
projections under the assumption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:24.560
that we do not do anything to slow the spread&nbsp;
of this virus. Or we'll consider sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:28.800
the opposite extreme. We'll assume we're&nbsp;
staying strict for the foreseeable future.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:32.000
And then let's consider something in between,&nbsp;
where we're sort of on a roller coaster of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.760
trying to contain this virus. And the forecast&nbsp;
under those scenarios looked very different.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.160
There's a lot of misunderstanding&nbsp;
about the application of models.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.440
What I'd love to see the public take away from&nbsp;
our modeling for COVID is that this is one tool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:00.720
in the toolkit. And it's really important for&nbsp;
us all to understand how the tools get used.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:05.200
Nobody knew anything about&nbsp;
epidemiological modeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:09.920
12 months ago. All of a sudden we're seeing&nbsp;
the results of modeling on the nightly news.&nbsp;

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:19.520
I find it fascinating and really pretty&nbsp;
awe-inspiring that there's this huge uptick in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.720
scientific awareness and awareness of&nbsp;
what mathematical modeling even is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:30.240
and, I think, a new appreciation&nbsp;
for the fact that it's out there.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:34.960
Pandemics will come again. We are&nbsp;
in a relationship with nature that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:39.600
favors that pathogens jump from one&nbsp;
species to another. So for the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:44.160
what I envision is something similar to&nbsp;
what we currently do with the weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:48.320
forecast. We have whole institutions,&nbsp;
we have surveillance systems with many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:54.880
weather stations all over our territories that&nbsp;
inform us of the current conditions everywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:10:01.120
And this is fed into models that continuously&nbsp;
update their forecasts about weather. Why don't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.840
we have that for pandemics? This is not a matter&nbsp;
of a single country. We have to be together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:13.600
in this. So when we do face a new challenge—&nbsp;
and they will come—then we are ready just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:31.840
plug in the new data that instantly arrives&nbsp;
and say something about how this can spread.

